E.append(pw("pathway-anterior-spinocerebellar", "Anterior spinocerebellar tract", "ascending", "proprioceptive and interneuronal feedback from the lower limb",
 "Neurons at the base of the dorsal horn and in the intermediate gray of the lumbosacral cord send axons that mostly cross in the anterior white commissure, ascend in the anterolateral funiculus in front of the posterior spinocerebellar tract, pass through the medulla and pons, and enter the cerebellum through the superior cerebellar peduncle, where most cross back so that each side of the cerebellum still receives information from its own side of the body. It carries less pure proprioception and more information about the activity of spinal interneurons, telling the cerebellum what the spinal circuits are doing with the descending commands.",
 (["spinal-grey-posterior-horn"], "Spinal border cells and intermediate gray of the lumbosacral segments; input from Golgi tendon organs, flexor reflex afferents and descending tracts."),
 [("Dorsal root ganglion cell", "spinal border cells"), ("Spinal border cell", "cerebellar cortex of the anterior lobe (vermis and paravermis) via the superior cerebellar peduncle, crossing twice")],
 ("Anterior white commissure (crosses) and superior cerebellar peduncle (crosses back)", "spinal-white-columns", "Double crossing makes the net projection ipsilateral."),
 [("spinal-grey-posterior-horn", "spinal-cord", "ipsilateral", "spinal border cells, L1–S2"), ("spinal-white-columns", "spinal-cord", "contralateral", "anterolateral funiculus, anterior to the posterior spinocerebellar tract"), ("level-medulla-olive", "brainstem", "contralateral", "lateral medulla"), ("superior-cerebellar-peduncle", "superior-cerebellar-peduncle", "contralateral", "enters through the superior peduncle at the upper pons"), ("cerebellar-vermis", "cerebellar-vermis-vi", "ipsilateral", "anterior lobe, after recrossing")],
 "Cerebellar anterior lobe vermis and intermediate zone, ipsilateral to the origin after the second crossing.",
 [("Spinal cord anterolateral funiculus", "Ipsilateral leg ataxia (net effect after double crossing), usually mixed with spinothalamic signs", "ipsilateral"), ("Superior cerebellar peduncle", "Ataxia with the cerebellar outflow signs dominating", "ipsilateral")],
 ["This is the only spinocerebellar tract that enters through the superior peduncle", "It monitors spinal interneurons rather than joints: a copy of what the cord is doing"],
 [R("sp-spinocerebellar-dorsal-tract"), R("sp-cerebellum"), R("sp-spinal-cord-morphology")], meshes=["superior-cerebellar-peduncle"], syndromes=["syn-cerebellar-hemispheric"], synonyms=["ventral spinocerebellar tract", "Gowers' tract"]))

E.append(pw("pathway-rubrospinal", "Rubrospinal tract", "descending", "flexor facilitation of the upper limb",
 "Neurons of the magnocellular red nucleus send axons that cross immediately in the ventral tegmental decussation of the midbrain and descend in the lateral funiculus of the cord just in front of the lateral corticospinal tract, ending on interneurons that excite flexor and inhibit extensor motor neurons, mainly of the arm. Small in humans, it matters clinically in two ways: it is one reason cerebellar and red nucleus lesions affect limb movement, and its preserved flexor drive to the arms after lesions above the red nucleus produces decorticate posturing, while lesions below the red nucleus remove it and leave extensor (decerebrate) posturing.",
 (["red-nucleus"], "Magnocellular part of the red nucleus, driven by the motor cortex and the interposed nuclei of the cerebellum."),
 [("Red nucleus neuron", "spinal interneurons in the intermediate gray of the cervical cord"), ("Interneuron", "flexor motor neurons")],
 ("Ventral tegmental decussation, midbrain", "red-nucleus", "Crosses at once beneath the red nucleus."),
 [("red-nucleus", "red-nucleus-l", "ipsilateral", "origin"), ("level-midbrain-superior-colliculus", "brainstem", "contralateral", "ventral tegmental decussation"), ("level-pons-caudal", "brainstem", "contralateral", "lateral tegmentum"), ("spinal-white-columns", "spinal-cord", "contralateral", "lateral funiculus, anterior to the corticospinal tract, cervical segments mainly")],
 "Interneurons of the cervical cord facilitating flexors of the upper limb.",
 [("Above the red nucleus (hemispheric mass, thalamus)", "Decorticate posture: arms flexed, legs extended (rubrospinal flexor drive intact)", "bilateral"), ("Below the red nucleus (pons)", "Decerebrate posture: arms and legs extended (only vestibulospinal and reticulospinal extensor drive remains)", "bilateral")],
 ["The posture of a comatose patient tells you whether the red nucleus is still connected to the cord: flexed arms above, extended arms below", "In humans the corticospinal tract has taken over most of what the rubrospinal tract does in cats"],
 [R("sp-red-nucleus"), R("sp-decerebrate-and-decorticate-posturing")], meshes=["red-nucleus-l", "red-nucleus-r"], syndromes=["syn-central-transtentorial-herniation", "syn-claude"]))

E.append(pw("pathway-vestibulospinal", "Vestibulospinal tracts", "descending", "postural tone and balance",
 "Two tracts carry vestibular information to the cord. The lateral vestibulospinal tract from the lateral (Deiters) nucleus descends uncrossed the whole length of the cord in the anterior funiculus and excites extensor (antigravity) motor neurons, driven by the otoliths and by the cerebellar vermis and fastigial nucleus; the medial vestibulospinal tract from the medial nucleus descends in the MLF to the cervical cord, bilaterally, to coordinate head and neck position with eye movements. Together with the reticulospinal tracts they make up the medial (ventromedial) descending system for posture, and their unopposed extensor drive is what produces decerebrate rigidity.",
 (["vestibular-nuclei"], "Lateral vestibular nucleus (lateral tract) and medial vestibular nucleus (medial tract), receiving the vestibular nerve and cerebellar input."),
 [("Vestibular nuclear neuron", "spinal interneurons and directly on extensor motor neurons"), ("Motor neuron", "antigravity muscles")],
 None,
 [("vestibular-nuclei", "brainstem", "ipsilateral", "lateral and medial nuclei at the pontomedullary junction"), ("level-medulla-olive", "brainstem", "ipsilateral", "lateral tract in the ventrolateral medulla; medial tract in the MLF"), ("spinal-white-columns", "spinal-cord", "ipsilateral", "anterior funiculus; the medial tract ends in the cervical cord, the lateral runs to the sacral cord"), ("spinal-grey-anterior-horn", "spinal-cord", "ipsilateral", "extensor motor neurons and interneurons")],
 "Extensor motor neurons of the trunk and limbs (lateral tract) and neck motor neurons (medial tract, bilateral).",
 [("Vestibular nerve or nuclei (unilateral)", "Falling and past-pointing toward the side of the lesion, lateropulsion", "ipsilateral"), ("Brainstem transection between the vestibular nuclei and the red nucleus", "Decerebrate rigidity: unopposed extensor drive", "bilateral"), ("Cerebellar vermis (anterior lobe)", "Loss of inhibition of the lateral vestibular nucleus: increased extensor tone and gait ataxia", "bilateral")],
 ["The lateral vestibulospinal tract is the extensor engine of decerebrate posturing", "Patients with acute vestibular lesions fall toward the lesion; the tract has lost its push on that side"],
 [R("sp-vestibular-pathways"), R("sp-nucleus-vestibular"), R("sp-decerebrate-and-decorticate-posturing")], meshes=["brainstem"], syndromes=["syn-vestibular-neuritis", "syn-central-transtentorial-herniation"]))

E.append(pw("pathway-reticulospinal", "Reticulospinal tracts", "descending", "posture, locomotion, tone and autonomic modulation",
 "The pontine and medullary reticular formation send two tracts to the cord: the pontine (medial) reticulospinal tract, uncrossed in the anterior funiculus, which facilitates extensors and axial muscles, and the medullary (lateral) reticulospinal tract, partly crossed in the lateral funiculus, which inhibits extensor tone and modulates reflexes and pain transmission. Driven by the cortex, they form the main route for postural adjustments, anticipatory postural control and locomotor commands, and their imbalance after upper motor neuron lesions (loss of the cortical drive to the inhibitory medullary tract) contributes to spasticity more than the loss of the corticospinal tract itself.",
 (["reticular-formation"], "Pontine reticular nuclei (nucleus reticularis pontis oralis and caudalis) and the medullary gigantocellular reticular nucleus, driven by the premotor cortex."),
 [("Reticular formation neuron", "spinal interneurons and gamma and alpha motor neurons over many segments"), ("Motor neuron", "axial and proximal muscles")],
 ("Partly crossed (medullary tract)", None, "Pontine tract uncrossed; medullary tract bilateral."),
 [("reticular-formation", "brainstem", "ipsilateral", "pontine and medullary reticular nuclei"), ("level-medulla-olive", "brainstem", "bilateral", "medullary tract descends in the lateral tegmentum"), ("spinal-white-columns", "spinal-cord", "bilateral", "pontine tract in the anterior funiculus, medullary tract in the lateral funiculus"), ("spinal-grey-anterior-horn", "spinal-cord", "bilateral", "interneurons and motor neurons of axial and proximal muscles; also autonomic neurons")],
 "Interneurons and motor neurons throughout the cord, especially for axial and proximal muscles; sympathetic preganglionic neurons; dorsal horn (pain modulation).",
 [("Cortical or capsular lesion (loss of drive to the medullary inhibitory tract)", "Spasticity, clasp-knife tone and hyperreflexia below the lesion", "contralateral"), ("Pontine tegmental lesion", "Loss of anticipatory postural adjustments; contributes to decerebrate posturing when combined with vestibulospinal release", "bilateral"), ("Medullary lesion", "Loss of descending pain inhibition and of autonomic modulation (blood pressure lability)", "bilateral")],
 ["Spasticity is largely a reticulospinal problem: the corticospinal tract alone produces weakness and clumsiness, not hypertonia", "Reticulospinal pathways explain why patients with corticospinal lesions can still walk with a stiff gait: the medial descending systems drive locomotion"],
 [R("sp-reticular-formation"), R("sp-decerebrate-and-decorticate-posturing"), R("sp-spasticity")], meshes=["tract-reticulospinal-l", "tract-reticulospinal-r"], syndromes=["syn-complete-transection-spinal-shock", "syn-central-transtentorial-herniation"]))

E.append(pw("pathway-tectospinal", "Tectospinal tract", "descending", "reflex turning of the head toward stimuli",
 "Neurons of the deep layers of the superior colliculus send axons that cross in the dorsal tegmental decussation just below the periaqueductal gray, descend near the MLF through the brainstem and end in the cervical cord, where they drive neck motor neurons to turn the head toward a visual, auditory or somatosensory stimulus. It is the spinal component of the orienting response and works with the tectobulbar fibers that move the eyes toward the same target, so that gaze and head turn arrive together and the fovea lands on whatever attracted attention.",
 (["superior-colliculus"], "Deep and intermediate layers of the superior colliculus, driven by retinal, auditory and cortical input."),
 [("Superior colliculus neuron", "cervical interneurons and motor neurons"), ("Motor neuron", "neck muscles")],
 ("Dorsal tegmental decussation, midbrain", "superior-colliculus", "Crosses beneath the periaqueductal gray at the level of the colliculi."),
 [("superior-colliculus", "superior-colliculus-l", "ipsilateral", "origin"), ("level-midbrain-superior-colliculus", "brainstem", "contralateral", "dorsal tegmental decussation"), ("medial-longitudinal-fasciculus", "brainstem", "contralateral", "descends beside the MLF"), ("spinal-white-columns", "spinal-cord", "contralateral", "anterior funiculus, cervical segments only")],
 "Motor neurons and interneurons of the upper cervical cord for neck muscles.",
 [("Superior colliculus", "Impaired reflex orienting of the head and eyes to the contralateral side; rarely isolated", "contralateral"), ("Dorsal midbrain compression", "Orienting deficits merge with the vertical gaze palsy of the Parinaud syndrome", "bilateral")],
 ["This tract is why a sudden sound makes you turn your head before you know why", "Isolated tectospinal lesions are a textbook concept rather than a clinical entity; think of it as part of the dorsal midbrain"],
 [R("sp-tectospinal-tract")], meshes=["superior-colliculus-l", "superior-colliculus-r"], syndromes=["syn-parinaud-dorsal-midbrain"]))

write(E)
